Yes, an array combined with a dictionary (associative array) would be an efficient data structure to store and retrieve a list of unique users who have made purchases on an e-commerce website. 
The array would store the unique users while the dictionary would store the purchase date for each user. To ensure that the users are listed in chronological order based on the date of purchase, you can use the purchase date as the key for the dictionary. This way, when you retrieve the list of users, you can sort them based on their purchase date.
Here's an example code:
```
$users = array();
$purchaseDates = array();
// Adding a new user and the date of purchase
function addPurchase($user, $date) {
    global $users, $purchaseDates;
    if (!in_array($user, $users)) {
        $users[] = $user;
    }
    $purchaseDates[$date] = $user;
}
// Retrieving the list of users in chronological order
function getUsers() {
    global $users, $purchaseDates;
    ksort($purchaseDates);
    $result = array();
    foreach ($purchaseDates as $date => $user) {
        $result[] = $user;
    }
    return $result;
}
// Checking if a user exists and their purchase date
function getUserPurchaseDate($user) {
    global $purchaseDates;
    if (isset($purchaseDates[$user])) {
        return $purchaseDates[$user];
    } else {
        return null;
    }
}
// Removing a user and their purchase date
function removeUser($user) {
    global $users, $purchaseDates;
    if (($key = array_search($user, $users)) !== false) {
        unset($users[$key]);
        unset($purchaseDates[getUserPurchaseDate($user)]);
    }
}
```
