Here's an example C# program that assigns a score to each item in a list based on certain criteria without any item being valued more or less than another based on those criteria:
```
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // Define the list of items
        List<string> items = new List<string>() { "apple", "banana", "orange", "pear" };
        // Define the criteria
        Dictionary<string, int> criteria = new Dictionary<string, int>() {
            { "color", 10 },
            { "size", 10 },
            { "taste", 10 }
        };
        // Assign scores to each item based on the criteria
        Dictionary<string, int> scores = new Dictionary<string, int>();
        foreach (string item in items)
        {
            int score = 0;
            foreach (KeyValuePair<string, int> criterion in criteria)
            {
                score += criterion.Value;
            }
            scores.Add(item, score);
        }
        // Print the scores
        foreach (KeyValuePair<string, int> score in scores)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(score.Key + ": " + score.Value);
        }
    }
}
```
In this program, we define a list of items and a dictionary of criteria. We then iterate through each item in the list and calculate a score for it based on the criteria. Finally, we print out the scores for each item. Since all criteria are weighted equally, no item is valued more or less than another based on those criteria.

