To modify the existing C++ program to determine the top N longest rivers in the world based on their length, you can use the following steps:
1. Create a data structure to store the river names and their respective lengths. You can use an array of structures or a vector of structures for this purpose.
2. Read the river names and their respective lengths from a file or from user input and store them in the data structure.
3. Sort the rivers by length in descending order. You can use a sorting algorithm such as bubble sort, selection sort, insertion sort, or quicksort. For example, to sort the rivers in descending order by length using bubble sort, you can use the following code:
```c++
struct River {
    string name;
    double length;
};
void sortRiversDescending(River rivers[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (rivers[j].length < rivers[j + 1].length) {
                River temp = rivers[j];
                rivers[j] = rivers[j + 1];
                rivers[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
```
4. Display the top N longest rivers in the world based on their length. You can use a loop to iterate through the sorted data structure and display the name and length of each river. For example, to display the top 5 longest rivers, you can use the following code:
```c++
void displayTopNRivers(River rivers[], int n) {
    cout << "Top " << n << " longest rivers in the world:" << endl;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cout << i + 1 << ". " << rivers[i].name << " (" << rivers[i].length << " km)" << endl;
    }
}
```
As for the formula to sort the rivers by length, you can use any of the sorting algorithms mentioned above. However, the basic idea is to compare the lengths of two rivers and swap them if they are in the wrong order (i.e., if the first river is shorter than the second river in descending order). The sorting algorithm will repeat this comparison and swapping process until the rivers are sorted in descending order by length.

