Here's the implementation of the `createBinarySearchTree` function in Go:
```
func createBinarySearchTree(array []int) *TreeNode {
    if len(array) == 0 {
        return nil
    }
    root := &TreeNode{value: array[0]}
    for _, v := range array[1:] {
        insertNode(root, v)
    }
    return root
}
func insertNode(root *TreeNode, value int) {
    if value < root.value {
        if root.left == nil {
            root.left = &TreeNode{value: value}
        } else {
            insertNode(root.left, value)
        }
    } else if value > root.value {
        if root.right == nil {
            root.right = &TreeNode{value: value}
        } else {
            insertNode(root.right, value)
        }
    }
}
```
This function first creates the root node with the first element of the array. Then, it iterates over the rest of the elements in the array and inserts them into the tree using the `insertNode` function. The `insertNode` function compares the value to be inserted with the value of the current node and recursively inserts it into the left or right subtree of the current node depending on whether the value is lesser or greater than the current node's value.
