Evaluation metrics for multi-label classification models include precision, recall, F1-score, and Hamming loss. Precision measures the proportion of true positives among the predicted positives, while recall measures the proportion of true positives among the actual positives. F1-score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, and is a measure of the model's overall performance. Hamming loss measures the fraction of incorrectly predicted labels.
In real-world scenarios, multi-label classification models can be used in recommendation systems to suggest relevant products or services to users based on their interests and preferences. They can also be used in customer support to classify customer queries and route them to the appropriate department for resolution. Additionally, they can be used in content filtering to classify text documents and filter out inappropriate or irrelevant content.

